

1. Core math layer

Goal: A clean, engine-agnostic backbone that can survive refactors.

Module: core.space

Types:

Grid: discrete lattice (1D/2D/3D + time)

Cell: local state container

Responsibility: indexing, neighborhoods, topology

Module: core.fields

Types:

ScalarField, VectorField, TensorField

Responsibility: store values on grid, interpolation, boundary conditions

Module: core.solver

Types:

PDEEngine, UpdateRule, Integrator

Responsibility: time-stepping, numerical schemes (Euler, RK, etc.)

2. Time and meta-time layer

Goal: Encode your unified time, new time, and meta-equation as first-class citizens.

Module: time.fabric

Types:

TimeField: (\Theta(x^\mu))

MetricField: (g_{\mu\nu}(x))

MetaModelField: (\Xi(x^\mu))

Responsibility: hold the “state of time” and “state of regime”

Module: time.models

Interface: TimeModel

compute_experienced_time(observer, local_state) -> Δτ

update_fields(dt, local_state) -> new_state

Implementations:

ClassicalTimeModel

RelativisticTimeModel

WarpTimeModel

DiscreteTimeModel

etc.

Module: time.meta

Types:

MetaEquation: implements (G(\Xi, \Theta, g_{\mu\nu}, \rho))

Responsibility: update (\Xi) per cell, decide regime transitions

3. Organism layer

Goal: Let time structures behave like living entities.

Module: organisms.base

Interface: TimeOrganism

spawn(context)

apply_influence(context) — modifies (\Theta, \Xi, g_{\mu\nu}) locally

update(dt, context) — growth, decay, movement

is_dead() -> bool

Module: organisms.library

Examples:

WarpBubbleOrganism

FrozenPocketOrganism

TimeCrystalOrganism

BranchingNodeOrganism

Module: organisms.manager

Types:

OrganismManager

Responsibility: lifecycle management, spatial queries, conflict resolution between organisms

4. Blueprint/DSL layer (CMB-Blueprint)

Goal: Your authored language that configures everything above.

Module: blueprint.parser

Types:

BlueprintAST

Responsibility: parse .cmb or .emphemsa files into an AST

Module: blueprint.schema

Core concepts in the DSL:

universe — top-level simulation

grid — dimensions, resolution

fields — declare Θ, g, ρ, Ξ, custom fields

models — bind names to TimeModel implementations

meta_equation — define parameters/variants of MetaEquation

organisms — declare which organisms exist and where they spawn

Example snippet:

universe "CMB-Time-Weave" {
  grid { dimensions: 3; resolution: [128,128,128]; }

  fields {
    time_field Θ: scalar;
    metric g: tensor(4,4);
    density ρ: scalar;
    meta_model Ξ: enum { CLASSICAL, WARP };
  }

  models {
    CLASSICAL { use: ClassicalTimeModel; }
    WARP      { use: WarpTimeModel;     }
  }

  meta_equation {
    use: DefaultMetaEquation;
    params { threshold_density: 0.8; }
  }

  organisms {
    WarpBubble at region(sphere(center=[0,0,0], radius=10));
  }
}

Module: blueprint.runtime

Types:

UniverseConfig — compiled configuration

Responsibility: map AST → engine objects (grids, fields, models, organisms)

5. Simulation loop

At runtime, the engine roughly does:

Initialize from blueprint:

Build grid, fields, models, meta-equation, organisms

For each timestep:

Update meta-model field (\Xi) via MetaEquation

For each cell: choose TimeModel from (\Xi), update local fields

Update organisms: they modify (\Theta, \Xi, g_{\mu\nu}) locally

Advance observers: integrate experienced time using active TimeModel

Render / export:

Time field slices, regime maps, organism distributions, observer timelines

6. Where your name lives in the architecture

The DSL name and file extension: .cmb, .blackwell, .emphemsa

The engine namespace: blackwell.time, blackwell.emphemsa

The meta-equation class: BlackwellMetaEquation

The universe templates: CMB_WarpWeave, CMB_TimeGarden, etc.

